WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.224
You built an app.

00:00:01.224 --> 00:00:02.449
Your friends love it.

00:00:02.449 --> 00:00:04.285
Does that mean customers will pay?

00:00:04.285 --> 00:00:08.265
Not yet - you have evidence of interest, but not of paying demand.

00:00:08.265 --> 00:00:10.713
This lesson is about what a business is.

00:00:10.713 --> 00:00:13.468
Customer → offer → delivery; interest vs paying demand.

00:00:13.468 --> 00:00:15.611
Do not treat this as theory only.

00:00:15.611 --> 00:00:18.672
Treat it as a move you can run this week.

00:00:18.992 --> 00:00:22.824
A business provides something customers value in exchange for payment.

00:00:22.824 --> 00:00:27.805
To understand how it works, start with three parts: customer, offer, and delivery.

00:00:27.805 --> 00:00:32.019
Remember the objective: identify customer, offer, delivery in a simple example.

00:00:32.019 --> 00:00:36.234
Write the idea in your own words before you move on.

00:00:36.234 --> 00:00:40.832
Name one concrete next action you can finish in under an hour.

00:00:41.152 --> 00:00:43.991
The customer is the person or organization buying.

00:00:43.991 --> 00:00:47.184
Ask: who are they, and what do they need?

00:00:47.184 --> 00:00:51.087
Remember the objective: identify customer, offer, delivery in a simple example.

00:00:51.087 --> 00:00:54.990
Write the idea in your own words before you move on.

00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:59.248
Name one concrete next action you can finish in under an hour.

00:00:59.568 --> 00:01:06.090
The offer is what they can buy: a clear product or service, its price, and what is included.

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:10.075
Remember the objective: identify customer, offer, delivery in a simple example.

00:01:10.075 --> 00:01:14.060
Write the idea in your own words before you move on.

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:18.408
Name one concrete next action you can finish in under an hour.

00:01:18.728 --> 00:01:21.651
Delivery is how they receive what you promised.

00:01:21.651 --> 00:01:25.671
Payment can happen before or after delivery, depending on the arrangement.

00:01:25.671 --> 00:01:29.691
Remember the objective: identify customer, offer, delivery in a simple example.

00:01:29.691 --> 00:01:33.711
Write the idea in your own words before you move on.

00:01:33.711 --> 00:01:38.096
Name one concrete next action you can finish in under an hour.

00:01:38.416 --> 00:01:42.913
For example, Maya sells a seventy-nine-dollar invoicing guide to freelance designers.

00:01:42.913 --> 00:01:47.411
Remember the objective: identify customer, offer, delivery in a simple example.

00:01:47.411 --> 00:01:51.908
Write the idea in your own words before you move on.

00:01:51.908 --> 00:01:56.815
Name one concrete next action you can finish in under an hour.

00:01:56.815 --> 00:02:01.312
Avoid vanity work that feels busy but does not create proof.

00:02:01.632 --> 00:02:05.346
Her customers are freelance designers who need help organizing invoices.

00:02:05.346 --> 00:02:08.317
Her offer is the guide for seventy-nine dollars.

00:02:08.317 --> 00:02:10.917
She delivers it through a download link.

00:02:10.917 --> 00:02:15.002
Remember the objective: identify customer, offer, delivery in a simple example.

00:02:15.002 --> 00:02:19.087
Write the idea in your own words before you move on.

00:02:19.087 --> 00:02:23.544
